1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,849
Research rarely fits in one tab.

2
00:00:02,849 --> 00:00:11,557
In our ten-tab test, browse used fifty-nine percent less RAM than
Chrome, leaving more memory for the rest of your work.

3
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:21,122
Less RAM, more room for research, and potentially lower energy use
when reduced memory pressure means less swapping.

4
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:24,542
Command L brings up search.

5
00:00:24,542 --> 00:00:29,844
Pick up a recent query, or explore fresh Google suggestions as you
type.

6
00:00:29,844 --> 00:00:32,383
Both options are yours to turn on.

7
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:39,447
Already have a source open? Command K finds your tabs.

8
00:00:39,447 --> 00:00:44,817
Type part of the title, choose your page, and jump back into the
research.

9
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:49,660
Compare sources with Command Shift right bracket.

10
00:00:49,660 --> 00:00:54,122
Left bracket brings you back, with the highlight following your place.

11
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:01,329
Within a tab, Command right bracket moves forward in page history.

12
00:01:01,329 --> 00:01:03,948
Command left bracket takes you back.

13
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:10,019
As you scroll, the tab shows how far you have read.

14
00:01:10,019 --> 00:01:13,643
Revisit a section, and the progress follows you back.

15
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:20,532
Codegraff sits beside the page.

16
00:01:20,532 --> 00:01:27,165
Ask about what you are reading, explore a question, or get help with a
form, without leaving your work.

17
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:35,086
When it is time to focus, Command S lets the tabs step aside.

18
00:01:35,086 --> 00:01:37,554
More room for your research.

19
00:01:37,554 --> 00:01:39,383
Browse, for the Mac.
